\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Scope.}
All experiments use Qwen3 (0.6B and 1.7B) under self-contamination with 132--200M tokens of continued pretraining.
Generalization to other architectures, full pretraining scale ($>$100B tokens), or realistic multi-source web contamination---where generator--corpus distribution mismatch may reduce detector accuracy---remains untested.

\item \textbf{Detector coverage.}
Only three detectors from two families (discriminative TF-IDF+LR and generative perplexity scorers) were evaluated; fine-tuned neural classifiers (e.g., ModernBERT) and zero-shot methods (e.g., DetectGPT, Binoculars) remain untested, so the AUC/ECE thresholds reported here may shift with broader detector coverage.
\end{itemize}

tool_args.old_string:
\section*{Ethics Statement}
All experiments use publicly available datasets (FineWeb) and open-weight models; no human subjects are involved.
Synthetic text detectors and provenance-based reweighting may misclassify human-authored text as synthetic; real-world web-scale deployment would require a calibration audit to bound false-positive rates before suppressing training signal.
We will release training scripts, detector scores, configuration files, and evaluation commands upon acceptance.

\bibliography{references}

\paragraph{Calibration, not just discrimination.}
Replacing TF-IDF+LR with generative perplexity detectors reverses the advantage across three seeds (GPT-2: AUC\,=\,0.91, ECE\,=\,0.131, $\Delta{=}{+}0.098$; N-gram: AUC\,=\,0.73, ECE\,=\,0.069, $\Delta{=}{+}0.129$; Figure~\ref{fig:detector_req}; Appendix~\ref{app:detector}).
The TF-IDF vs.\ GPT-2 contrast (comparable AUC, $2.7{\times}$ ECE gap) isolates calibration as an empirically observed requirement in our self-contamination setting; N-gram's better ECE (0.069) does not compensate for insufficient AUC (0.73), confirming that both discrimination and calibration are required.

